    "q": "<p>Japan's PPF is x/24 + y/8 = 1 and India's PPF is x/36 + y/6 = 1 (x = wheat, y = cars).</p><ol><li>Who has the comparative advantage in each good? Who has the absolute advantage?</li><li>What should each country produce, and what is the total output?</li><li>Compare with the case where each country uses half its resources on each good.</li></ol>",
    "a": "<p>Opportunity costs:</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Japan</th><th>India</th></tr><tr><td>One wheat</td><td>8/24 = 1/3 car</td><td>6/36 = 1/6 car</td></tr><tr><td>One car</td><td>24/8 = 3 wheat</td><td>36/6 = 6 wheat</td></tr></table><p><b>1.</b> Wheat: India 1/6 &lt; Japan 1/3 → <b>India</b> has the comparative advantage in wheat. Cars: Japan 3 &lt; India 6 → <b>Japan</b> in cars. Absolute advantage: India in wheat (36 &gt; 24), Japan in cars (8 &gt; 6).</p><p><b>2.</b> Japan makes only cars (8), India only wheat (36). Total: <b>36 wheat + 8 cars</b>.</p><p><b>3.</b> Half and half: Japan (12 wheat, 4 cars), India (18 wheat, 3 cars). Total 30 wheat + 7 cars. Specialization gives <b>6 more wheat and 1 more car</b>.</p>"
