    "a": "<p>Germany has the <b>absolute advantage</b> in both goods (40 &gt; 30 grapes, 20 &gt; 5 machines). But trade depends on <b>comparative</b> advantage = lower opportunity cost.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Germany</th><th>Uzbekistan</th></tr><tr><td>One grape</td><td>20/40 = 1/2 machine</td><td>5/30 = 1/6 machine</td></tr><tr><td>One machine</td><td>40/20 = 2 grapes</td><td>30/5 = 6 grapes</td></tr></table><p>Grapes: Uzbekistan 1/6 &lt; Germany 1/2 → <b>Uzbekistan</b> has the comparative advantage in grapes. Machines: Germany 2 &lt; Uzbekistan 6 → <b>Germany</b> in machines.</p><p>So the student is <b>wrong</b>. Germany should specialize in machines (20) and Uzbekistan in grapes (30): total 30 grapes + 20 machines. Each country has a comparative advantage in something, so both can gain from trade.</p>"
