    "q": "<p>Egypt's PPF is x/50 + y/10 = 1 and Taiwan's PPF is x/20 + y/10 = 1 (x = cotton, y = computers). Without trade Egypt consumes (25 cotton, 5 computers) and Taiwan consumes (12 cotton, 4 computers). They specialize, and Taiwan gives 5 computers to Egypt for 15 cotton.</p><ol><li>Who specializes in what?</li><li>Is the deal acceptable to both?</li><li>Find what each country has after trade and how much each gains.</li></ol>",
    "a": "<p><b>1.</b> 1 computer costs Egypt 50/10 = 5 cotton and Taiwan 20/10 = 2 cotton. 1 cotton costs Egypt 1/5 computer and Taiwan 1/2 computer. Egypt has the comparative advantage in <b>cotton</b> (makes 50), Taiwan in <b>computers</b> (makes 10).</p><p><b>2.</b> Taiwan gives 5 computers, needs at least 5 × 2 = 10 cotton, gets 15 ✓. Egypt gives 15 cotton, needs at least 15 × 1/5 = 3 computers, gets 5 ✓. Price 3 cotton per computer, between 2 and 5 → acceptable.</p><p><b>3.</b></p><table><tr><th></th><th>Before (cotton, computers)</th><th>After</th><th>Point test</th><th>Gain</th></tr><tr><td>Egypt</td><td>(25, 5)</td><td>(50 − 15, 5) = (35, 5)</td><td>35/50 + 5/10 = 1.2 &gt; 1</td><td>+10 cotton</td></tr><tr><td>Taiwan</td><td>(12, 4)</td><td>(15, 10 − 5) = (15, 5)</td><td>15/20 + 5/10 = 1.25 &gt; 1</td><td>+3 cotton, +1 computer</td></tr></table><p>Both after-trade bundles are outside their own PPFs, so both are better off. Check: 35 + 15 = 50 cotton, 5 + 5 = 10 computers, exactly what was produced.</p>"
