    "q": "<p>A café has <b>one kazan</b> (fixed). With 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 cooks it makes 0, 40, 70, 90, 100, 105 plates of plov a day. (a) Find the marginal product of each cook. (b) Are there diminishing returns? Why? (c) Sketch the total product curve and describe its shape. (d) A 6th cook would leave output at 105 plates. What is his marginal product, and what does it mean for the TP curve?</p>",
    "a": "<p><b>(a)</b> MPL = ΔQ/ΔL: 40 − 0 = <b>40</b>; 70 − 40 = <b>30</b>; 90 − 70 = <b>20</b>; 100 − 90 = <b>10</b>; 105 − 100 = <b>5</b>.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Yes. Holding the kazan fixed, each extra cook adds less: 40 &gt; 30 &gt; 20 &gt; 10 &gt; 5. Diminishing returns = marginal product is decreasing. The reason: more cooks share the same kazan.</p><p><b>(c)</b> X axis: cooks (variable input); y axis: plates; the kazan is held constant. The curve rises (0, 40, 70, 90, 100, 105) but gets <b>flatter</b>, because each step up is smaller.</p><p><b>(d)</b> MP of the 6th cook = 105 − 105 = <b>0</b>. When MP = 0, total product stops rising: TP is at its top. (If MP became negative, TP would fall.)</p>"
