    "q": "<p>True or false? Give a reason.</p><ol><li>\"Diminishing returns\" means that total output falls when the firm hires more workers.</li><li>If the production function is Q = f(L) with f′(L) &gt; 0 and f″(L) &lt; 0, output rises with labor but more and more slowly.</li><li>The long run means any period longer than one year.</li></ol>",
    "a": "<ol><li><b>False.</b> Diminishing returns means the <b>marginal</b> product of an input falls when that input increases and other inputs are fixed. Total output can still rise, just by less each time (farm example: MPL 19, 17, 15 … 5, all positive).</li><li><b>True.</b> f′(L) is the slope of the TP curve = marginal product. f′(L) &gt; 0: each extra worker adds output, so TP rises. f″(L) &lt; 0: marginal product is falling, so TP gets flatter. This is diminishing returns.</li><li><b>False.</b> The long run is defined by inputs, not by time on a calendar: it is the period in which <b>all</b> inputs can be varied. The short run is when at least one input is fixed.</li></ol>"
