    "a": "<p><b>(a)</b> AFC = 90/Q; ATC = AFC + AVC.</p><table><tr><th>Q</th><th>AFC</th><th>AVC</th><th>ATC</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>90.00</td><td>10</td><td>100.00</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>45.00</td><td>20</td><td>65.00</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>30.00</td><td>30</td><td><b>60.00</b></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>22.50</td><td>40</td><td>62.50</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>18.00</td><td>50</td><td>68.00</td></tr></table><p><b>(b)</b></p><ul><li>1→2: AFC −45, AVC +10 → spreading wins, ATC falls (100 → 65).</li><li>2→3: AFC −15, AVC +10 → spreading wins, ATC falls (65 → 60).</li><li>3→4: AFC −7.50, AVC +10 → diminishing returns wins, ATC rises (60 → 62.50).</li><li>4→5: AFC −4.50, AVC +10 → diminishing returns wins, ATC rises (62.50 → 68).</li></ul><p><b>(c)</b> At low output the fixed cost is spread over more units and AFC drops a lot, so ATC falls. Later AFC hardly falls, while each unit needs more variable input, so AVC rising dominates and ATC rises. Falling, then rising = <b>U-shape</b> (bottom at Q = 3, ATC = $60).</p>"
