    "q": "<p>Prove that the MC curve crosses the ATC curve at the minimum of ATC. Use the derivative of ATC = TC/Q, and explain the result in words.</p>",
    "a": "<p>ATC = TC/Q. Using the quotient rule (and dTC/dQ = MC):</p><p>dATC/dQ = (MC·Q − TC)/Q<sup>2</sup> = (1/Q)(MC − TC/Q) = <b>(1/Q)(MC − ATC)</b></p><p>Since 1/Q &gt; 0, the slope of ATC has the same sign as MC − ATC:</p><ul><li>MC &lt; ATC → slope &lt; 0 → ATC falling.</li><li>MC &gt; ATC → slope &gt; 0 → ATC rising.</li><li>MC = ATC → slope = 0 → ATC at its <b>minimum</b>.</li></ul><p>In words: a new unit below the average pulls the average down, a new unit above it pulls the average up. So ATC is lowest exactly where MC = ATC (point M). The same proof with VC instead of TC shows MC also crosses AVC at its minimum.</p>"
