    "a": "<p>A <b>perfectly competitive market</b> is a market in which <b>all</b> participants, both consumers and producers, are <b>price-takers</b>. A <b>perfectly competitive industry</b> is an industry in which the <b>producers</b> are price-takers.</p><p>A price-taking producer (consumer) is one whose actions have <b>no effect</b> on the market price of the good it sells (buys).</p><p>Difference: \"market\" covers buyers <b>and</b> sellers; \"industry\" covers only the producers.</p><p>No single buyer or seller sets the price: it is determined by <b>industry demand and industry supply</b> together. Each firm just accepts it, so its demand curve is horizontal at the market price (P = MR).</p>"
