    "a": "<ol><li><b>False.</b> The two necessary conditions are (1) no producer has a large market share and (2) the good is a standardized product. Free entry and exit (new firms can easily enter, old firms can easily leave) is listed separately; it is typical of perfect competition and important in the long run.</li><li><b>False.</b> It can already sell all it wants at the market price, so cutting the price only lowers revenue. Its demand is a flat line at the market price.</li><li><b>True.</b> With a 60% share, it can cut output, create a shortage and push the price up, so it is not a price-taker.</li><li><b>False.</b> A price-taking consumer's actions have no effect on the market price; he or she is too small compared with the market.</li></ol>"
